# How git handles line endings in this repo.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# This project gets edited in two places: on Windows, where editors write
# CRLF line endings, and in GitHub's web editor, which writes LF. Without
# this file git stores whatever it is given, so the same file saved in each
# place looks like a total rewrite -- "197 insertions, 197 deletions" on a
# file where nothing actually changed.
#
# That is not just noise. A real one-line fix hidden inside a 197-line
# phantom diff is a fix nobody reviews, and `git add -A` sweeps the whole
# rewrite into a commit as though it were deliberate.
#
# `text=auto` tells git to store every text file with LF internally and hand
# Windows back CRLF on checkout. Both editors carry on behaving normally and
# the diffs show only what changed.
#
# The binary lines below are belt and braces. Git detects binaries on its own,
# but saying so outright guarantees no image, PDF or archive is ever put
# through line-ending conversion, which would corrupt it.

* text=auto

*.png  binary
*.webp binary
*.svg  text
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.b64  binary
